WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.610
Charles Bird King is our next stop.

2
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.510
He worked in Washington, D.C.,
right at the center of federal power,

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.820
and his main assignment was

4
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.300
to paint Native delegations visiting
the capital in the 1820s and 1830s:

5
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.990
men who were there to negotiate treaties,
defend territory,

6
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.970
and represent sovereign nations.

7
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.170
King's portraits became the core
of the government's Indian Gallery,

8
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.270
displayed in the War Department itself.

9
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.130
There's a tension here
that's hard to miss.

10
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.330
These were active political actors,
negotiating in real time.

11
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.500
Yet King's style nudges them
toward an earlier age,

12
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.590
as though they belong to a world
on the verge of disappearance.
